Exactly. Part of the value of owning something is having a life that gives you opportunities to enjoy it.

A beautiful suit means something different when you have occasions worth dressing up for. A sports car is more enjoyable when you have somewhere exciting to drive. A nice watch becomes part of the memories you make while wearing it.

And there’s an interesting reversal:

Sometimes you buy nice things because you have a lifestyle. Other times, you build a lifestyle because you want to use the nice things you have.

You don’t necessarily need to wait for an occasion, either. You can create one.

Wear the nice clothes to an ordinary dinner. Take the scenic route. Invite friends over and use the good dishes. Make a Tuesday worth remembering.

There’s a distinction between possessing something valuable and extracting value from possessing it.

And it extends beyond material things. Strength needs adventures. Knowledge needs application. Love needs expression. Talent needs an outlet.

Don’t just acquire the things you want in life. Build a life in which you actually get to use them.

Otherwise, you can end up owning all the equipment for a game you never play. ;)